    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from the Tieton River, whose name means “roaring water.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tieton%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1910 a federal Reclamation Act project brought irrigation, and by 1917 the Northern Pacific Railway linked Tieton to markets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.historylink.org/File/20615"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A 2007 revitalization plan called “Mighty Tieton” was launched by a Seattle lawyer to attract new industries.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tieton%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Tieton’s historic library, housed in city hall, closed in 2025 after federal funding cuts.",
